101
00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:11.810
Gary: You need to…

102
00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:20.259
Gary: You need to have the hash collection, and someone can find a contract address which conlitz one of these 20 accounts.

103
00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:21.730
Gary: So…

104
00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:34.179
Gary: The original idea of this EIP is, whenever we try to make the contract deployment, we always check if the… we always check if the storage is empty or not.

105
00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:47.350
Gary: But if it's feasible with this hash collection, it means the attacker can also derive a private key corresponding to any Ethereum address.

106
00:12:47.630 --> 00:12:54.690
Gary: Because they rely on the same security model, like the 100… 60Hz security.

107
00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:07.860
Gary: it will be a larger issue for the Ethereum. Like, the whole Ethereum security rely on this assumption. And if it's broken, it makes no sense to only protect the contract deployment.

108
00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:11.810
Gary: So in this regard, I think…

109
00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:17.980
